“Hands-On Cybersecurity Investigations (TryHackMe)”
Summary of incident

At 09:32 CDT on September 8th, 2026, we received a High-severity alert indicating that a user had clicked a link contained
in a suspected phishing email. The email appeared to impersonate Microsoft and directed the user to a fake login page.
The incident was escalated to the SOC Team Lead for further investigation.

Alert details

Alert Name: Suspected Phishing Link
Alert Source: Microsoft Sentinel
Severity: High
User Account: T.Ross
Host: WIN CORP A12
Time Window: 09:32–09:41 CDT
Event Count: 1 suspicious email, 1 link click
Source IP: 10.23.16.25
User’s Normal Location: Rochester, MN

Initial triage

Using the SIEM and email security logs, I was able to determine that the user received an email containing a suspicious link.
The destination domain was recently registered and did not match the legitimate Microsoft domain.

I also contacted the user through internal messaging. The user confirmed that they had clicked the link but did not enter
their password or MFA information.

Investigation findings

Phishing email successfully reached the user's inbox

User clicked the malicious link

Destination website was identified as a credential-harvesting page

No credentials were entered

No successful authentication from the malicious infrastructure was observed

No evidence of malware execution was found on the endpoint

Containment actions

Removed the phishing email from affected mailboxes

Blocked the malicious domain

Added the sender to the email security block list

Reviewed the user's recent authentication activity

Recovery & remediation

Confirmed that the user's credentials had not been submitted

Added the malicious domain to SIEM monitoring

Updated the phishing detection rule to identify similar messages

Provided the user with additional phishing-awareness guidance

Final assessment

This was a phishing attempt that resulted in a user clicking a malicious link. There was no evidence that credentials were 
submitted or that the attacker gained access to the user's account. The malicious domain and email were blocked, and 
additional monitoring was implemented to detect similar activity.

